From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 12:27:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_core: don't drop a config transition in
 hci_power_on()

A configuration change -- MGMT_OP_SET_PUBLIC_ADDRESS or
MGMT_OP_SET_EXTERNAL_CONFIG -- calls mgmt_index_removed(), sets both
HCI_CONFIG and HCI_AUTO_OFF, and re-queues the power_on work, relying on
it to announce the controller again via the

	else if (hci_dev_test_and_clear_flag(hdev, HCI_CONFIG))
		mgmt_index_added(hdev);

tail of hci_power_on().

But HCI_AUTO_OFF is also what selects the already-powered fast path at
the top of that function, which returns early. When the controller is
already up -- exactly the case after a configuration change -- the
transition is therefore dropped: the index is removed and never added
back, and HCI_CONFIG stays set forever. Since mgmt_control() rejects
every command while HCI_CONFIG is set, the controller answers Invalid
Index for the rest of its life and BlueZ can never adopt it.

Seen on a Pixel 2 XL (WCN3990 on UART). The controller initialises
perfectly on every boot and hci0 is UP RUNNING with real traffic
counters, but:

  btmgmt info            -> "Index list with 0 items"
  btmgmt --index 0 info  -> Invalid Index (0x11)
  btmon from boot        -> Read Controller Index List returns
                            Controllers: 0, no Index Added ever arrives

Instrumenting hci_power_on() showed it running twice, the second time
with setup=0 config=1 and taking the already-up branch:

  power_on entry: up=0 mgmt=0 auto_off=1 setup=1 config=0 bdaddr=00:00:..
  after open:     err=0 up=1 setup=1              bdaddr=<bdaddr>
  power_on entry: up=1 mgmt=1 auto_off=1 setup=0 config=1

Reloading the transport driver appeared to "fix" it only because
hci_register_dev() then runs again with no configuration change in
flight.

Move the setup/config completion into a helper and call it from both
exits, so the transition is honoured whether or not the controller was
already powered. No functional change for the paths that already worked.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 net/bluetooth/hci_core.c | 73 +++++++++++++++++++++++++---------------
 1 file changed, 45 insertions(+), 28 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/bluetooth/hci_core.c b/net/bluetooth/hci_core.c
index 8ccec73dce45..8c59b8e419cc 100644
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_core.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_core.c
@@ -940,6 +940,49 @@ static const struct rfkill_ops hci_rfkill_ops = {
 	.set_block = hci_rfkill_set_block,
 };
 
+/* Complete a pending setup or configuration transition by announcing the
+ * controller to mgmt.
+ *
+ * Both exits of hci_power_on() have to do this. A configuration change
+ * (Set Public Address, Set External Config) calls mgmt_index_removed(),
+ * sets HCI_CONFIG *and* HCI_AUTO_OFF, and re-queues the power_on work,
+ * relying on it to add the index back. HCI_AUTO_OFF is also what selects
+ * the already-powered fast path below, so without this the transition is
+ * silently dropped: HCI_CONFIG stays set, mgmt_control() answers Invalid
+ * Index for the rest of the controller's life, and BlueZ never sees it.
+ */
+static void hci_power_on_complete_transition(struct hci_dev *hdev)
+{
+	if (hci_dev_test_and_clear_flag(hdev, HCI_SETUP)) {
+		/* For unconfigured devices, set the HCI_RAW flag so that
+		 * userspace can easily identify them.
+		 */
+		if (hci_dev_test_flag(hdev, HCI_UNCONFIGURED))
+			set_bit(HCI_RAW, &hdev->flags);
+
+		/* For fully configured devices, this will send the Index Added
+		 * event. For unconfigured devices, it will send Unconfigured
+		 * Index Added event.
+		 *
+		 * Devices with HCI_QUIRK_RAW_DEVICE are ignored and no event
+		 * will be send.
+		 */
+		mgmt_index_added(hdev);
+	} else if (hci_dev_test_and_clear_flag(hdev, HCI_CONFIG)) {
+		/* When the controller is now configured, then it is important
+		 * to clear the HCI_RAW flag.
+		 */
+		if (!hci_dev_test_flag(hdev, HCI_UNCONFIGURED))
+			clear_bit(HCI_RAW, &hdev->flags);
+
+		/* Powering on the controller with HCI_CONFIG set only happens
+		 * with the transition from unconfigured to configured. This
+		 * will send the Index Added event.
+		 */
+		mgmt_index_added(hdev);
+	}
+}
+
 static void hci_power_on(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct hci_dev *hdev = container_of(work, struct hci_dev, power_on);
@@ -953,6 +996,7 @@ static void hci_power_on(struct work_struct *work)
 		cancel_delayed_work(&hdev->power_off);
 		err = hci_powered_update_sync(hdev);
 		mgmt_power_on(hdev, err);
+		hci_power_on_complete_transition(hdev);
 		return;
 	}
 
@@ -979,34 +1023,7 @@ static void hci_power_on(struct work_struct *work)
 				   HCI_AUTO_OFF_TIMEOUT);
 	}
 
-	if (hci_dev_test_and_clear_flag(hdev, HCI_SETUP)) {
-		/* For unconfigured devices, set the HCI_RAW flag
-		 * so that userspace can easily identify them.
-		 */
-		if (hci_dev_test_flag(hdev, HCI_UNCONFIGURED))
-			set_bit(HCI_RAW, &hdev->flags);
-
-		/* For fully configured devices, this will send
-		 * the Index Added event. For unconfigured devices,
-		 * it will send Unconfigued Index Added event.
-		 *
-		 * Devices with HCI_QUIRK_RAW_DEVICE are ignored
-		 * and no event will be send.
-		 */
-		mgmt_index_added(hdev);
-	} else if (hci_dev_test_and_clear_flag(hdev, HCI_CONFIG)) {
-		/* When the controller is now configured, then it
-		 * is important to clear the HCI_RAW flag.
-		 */
-		if (!hci_dev_test_flag(hdev, HCI_UNCONFIGURED))
-			clear_bit(HCI_RAW, &hdev->flags);
-
-		/* Powering on the controller with HCI_CONFIG set only
-		 * happens with the transition from unconfigured to
-		 * configured. This will send the Index Added event.
-		 */
-		mgmt_index_added(hdev);
-	}
+	hci_power_on_complete_transition(hdev);
 }
 
 static void hci_power_off(struct work_struct *work)
